{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3097","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3097","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3097\",\"parent_revision\":\"f2b825ace49ab1110d5313afcc528da23ce964c1e8efc6e81f9d4c31460c69d9\",\"root_revision\":\"f2b825ace49ab1110d5313afcc528da23ce964c1e8efc6e81f9d4c31460c69d9\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item. The deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict) \u2014 not a mandate to ship the feature. Origin: comparison of graph-engineering research against the development-harness architecture, grounded against the #3060 agent over-orchestration incident (a task-worker recursively dispatched sub-teams after receiving orchestrator-voiced prose; ~$100 of unplanned spend). Shared design constraint across all seven exploration items in this set: the model keeps the judgment (scoring, declaring, deciding semantic dependencies) but writes it down ONCE, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists (sam_schema models, DependencyGraph, the implement-feature dispatch loop, work-milestone). No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 that larger direction is explicitly out of scope for these items.\\n\\nWhat/why: Model tier and effort for dispatched task-workers are currently chosen ad hoc at dispatch time. A rubric exists (.claude/rules/model-selection.md: haiku for checking, sonnet for implementation, opus for analysis; effort tiers low/medium/high/max) but it is applied by per-dispatch judgment, so application is inconsistent and cost is unmanaged. There is also no trigger that stops an oversized task from being dispatched to a single worker \u2014 a task too large for one context window gets a heroic single dispatch instead of decomposition.\\n\\nMitigates: inconsistent model/effort selection across dispatches; unbounded per-task cost; oversized tasks failing or thrashing in a single worker context.\\n\\nBenefit lands in: the implement-feature dispatch loop (mechanical tier selection), planning (swarm-task-planner emits the score once), and session cost (spend proportional to task complexity).\\n\\nProposed exploration: (1) add a complexity field to the SAM task model in sam_schema (score plus the rubric inputs: files-touched estimate, mechanical-vs-design, cross-module reach); (2) teach dh:swarm-task-planner to assign it at planning time using the model-selection rubric; (3) teach the implement-feature dispatch loop to read it mechanically \u2014 score maps to model tier + effort tier; score above a defined threshold routes to decompose-before-dispatch instead of single-worker dispatch; (4) run one real plan with scoring active and compare token spend and dispatch outcomes against a recent unscored plan of similar size.\\n\\nRelated: Item 7 (\\\"Explore selecting review depth (single review vs multi-perspective) from task complexity score\\\") consumes this score for verification fan-out. Item 4 (\\\"Explore per-task executor-type declaration so dispatch capability is planned, never inferred\\\") shares the \\\"judgment-as-planning-time-data\\\" mechanism.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Experiment or design exploration executed as described (prototype on a real plan, measured comparison, or written design verdict)\\n- [ ] Decision recorded with evidence: adopt, adapt, or reject \u2014 with rationale\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore complexity scoring on sam tasks to drive model tier, effort, and decomposition** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: graph-engineering research comparison\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"d09d959a8342378470165ee69351d9e6fc5111fda7455199c892bbed8c8f3b59\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIpL_g\"}"}